 overages and underages in supplements  are surprisingly  a common phenomenon one review recently  published in the journal of nutrition  showed that certain supplements tend to  have ingredient overages  of 20 percent or more specifically  a lot of multivitamins had approximately  10 to 20 percent more calcium  than their label had stated and unknown  to many  consuming too much calcium for some  people can cause  a number of adverse consequences like  constipation  and potentially kidney stones finally in  addition to discrepancies in ingredient  amounts  there's often the risk of actual  contaminants in supplements  where they ultimately do not belong  in 2010 u.s government accountability  office report  analyzed 40 different dietary  supplements looking for the presence of  lead  arsenic mercury cadmium or pesticides  the results showed that at least trace  amounts of at least one or more of these  contaminants  were found in 93 of those samples  in the case of botanicals there can be  huge variation  in strength and quality depending on the  genetic variation  or environmental conditions of the  plants of origin  one study reported that 59 of tested  botanical supplements  contained plant species not even listed  on the label  and that active ingredient substitution  was observed among  83 percent of companies tested  uh yikes so we have established  that supplements may or may not always  contain what they  claim to contain and may or may not  be healthy or safe for you depending on  a  wide range of unregulated factors but  what about how they're marketed to you  well turns out that can be a little bit  shady too  [Music]  in 1994 the dshea that we discussed  came up with structure function claims  so unlike drugs supplements are not  permitted to be marketed for the purpose  of  treating diagnosing preventing or curing  diseases so that's good so they can't  say that something  cures heart disease or lowers  cholesterol or reverses  erectile dysfunction what they can do  is use words like supports or targets  and that's why we often see supplements  with verbiage like  supports a healthy immune system or  targets our stress response  you may also notice that any claims that  they do make  need to be accompanied by a itty-bitty  little disclaimer  that these statements have not been  approved by the fda  so you've probably all seen that line a  lot  or if you haven't now you will now  before i get into how to choose a  reputable supplement i quickly want to  flag that  what i'm discussing largely is about  american regulations  i cannot speak to how supplements are  regulated in europe  or the uk and it's only slightly  different  here where i am in canada so  very quickly supplements in canada are  licensed as natural health products or  nhps which help to create safe product  formula standards  dosages and claims about usage nhps have  approved blurbs  about ingredients on packages that  include a little bit more detail about  ingredient types dosages and claims of  the product  there are also a few more details  provided about specific dietary  ingredients  also things like nutrients and other  substances that a supplement would have  on their label  and canadian regulations actually  license all of the businesses  involved in manufacturing holding  processing  and distributing a supplement finally  there's a legal  dietary ingredient database that exists  in canada where  all legitimate and licensed ingredients  are registered  unfortunately even with all of these  safeguards in place  this system is far from perfect  the standards are pretty low for what  passes as an effective supplement  marketing regulation is total crap  and there is a major backlog in adverse  event reports to health canada  so that it can take a very long time for  something to be  taken off the market so maybe not as bad  as the states  but still really not great so i know  that i may have  freaked you guys out a bit but i want to  remind you  that supplements are not bad and  supplement companies are  not all out to scam you or to make you  sick  absolutely not i think supplements are  vitally  important in a lot of cases it's just a  matter of finding the  right one for your needs so what should  you be aware of when  choosing your supplements number one  look for a third party tested product  i like to use websites like labdor  informed choice  and nsf sport to look for a product that  has been  tested by a company other than the one  that you're  buying it from to look for impurities  accuracy of labeling and  quality labdoor for example will send a  sample of each product to an fda  registered lab  for detailed chemical analysis which  includes  measurements of active ingredients and  potential contaminants  some sports supplements will include  certifications on their products  packaging such as  nsf or informed choice labels which are  intended to provide  assurance that the product has been  tested by respected  independent certification organizations  so that's something i recommend looking  for when you can  and if you can't find your go-to  supplement on one of these websites  you can email their support team and ask  to see  proof of their third-party testing  number two  use non-commercial websites to learn  about supplement ingredients  credible authorities like nih fda  and usda often have fact sheets  about common ingredients and their  respective evidence which  provides a more unbiased source on the  legitimacy  of some marketing claims compared to the  brand's websites  which we now know is not so well  regulated  number three know that if a claim sounds  too good to be true  it probably is so be mindful of product  claims such as  works better than a prescription drug or  it's 100  safe or has zero side effects if these  supplements have enough  of an effect on the body to work  they also have the potential for  unwanted side effects  next be aware that the term natural  doesn't always mean  safe so as we mentioned there's lots of  potential for interactions  and contraindications depending on your  unique health status  don't forget that supplements are never  meant to replace an  actual healthy diet or pharmaceuticals  when needed  so i always prefer to think of  supplements as complementary not  supplementary in the quest for good  health don't forget to  always speak to your health care  provider before starting  any supplement regimen and finally  report any serious side effects or  issues associated with a supplement  to the fda as soon as possible  your reporting could potentially save  someone else  or many people from serious harm by  getting an  unsafe or misleading product off the  shelves asap  in conclusion a reminder that i am not  insinuating that supplements are bad  i buy and i take a lot of supplements  but i also know what a wild west rodeo  it is out there  so i am sharing this information to help  you  become more informed consumers i'm  planning to hopefully make this into a  whole series where i look at different  kinds of supplements  and categorize them as yay or nay in my  books  so leave me a comment below if there's a  product that you take  that you want me to potentially review  and on that note  that is all that i have for you guys  today thanks again to skillshare for  sponsoring this video  if you liked it be sure to give it the  thumbs up leave me a comment below  subscribe to the channel and i'll see  you next time on abby's kitchen  [Music]  bye  [Music]  you
 

